## What `constexpr` really means

`constexpr` tells the compiler:

> *“This value or expression can be evaluated at compile‑time.”*

Once the compiler knows the value at compile time, it can use it in places that require a constant expression:

* array bounds (`int arr[10];`)
* non‑type template arguments (`template<int N> struct X; X<5> x;`)
* `static_assert` conditions
* initialization of `constexpr` variables, etc.

If the compiler *cannot* evaluate it at compile time (because the arguments are not known until run‑time), the function or variable can still be used at run‑time.  
So `constexpr` functions are **compile‑time friendly but fall back to run‑time** if needed.

### Quick checklist of what can be `constexpr`

| Feature | C++11 | C++14 | C++17 | C++20 |
|---------|-------|-------|-------|-------|
| `constexpr` variable (integral, literal type) | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ |
| `constexpr` function body | only `return` statements | loops, `if`, recursion | same | same |
| `constexpr` constructors (user‑defined types) | no default member initializers | yes | yes | yes |
| `new` / dynamic memory | ❌ | ❌ | ❌ | ❌ |
| `std::initializer_list` | ❌ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ |
| `constexpr` lambda | ❌ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ |

*(The table is simplified – for the full list see the C++ standard or a reference like cppreference.com.)*

---

## A simple, complete example

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <array>
#include <cassert>

/* 1. constexpr function (C++14) – recursive factorial */
constexpr unsigned int factorial(unsigned int n)
{
    return n <= 1 ? 1 : n * factorial(n - 1);
}

/* 2. constexpr variable – a compile‑time constant */
constexpr int max_size = factorial(5);   // 5! = 120

/* 3. constexpr class with a constexpr constructor and method */
struct Point
{
    int x, y;

    constexpr Point(int a, int b) : x(a), y(b) {}

    constexpr int sum() const { return x + y; }
};

int main()
{
    /* 4. Use constexpr variable as array size */
    std::array<int, max_size> arr{};          // array of 120 ints

    /* 5. Use constexpr function in static_assert (must be compile‑time) */
    static_assert(factorial(4) == 24, "Factorial wrong!");

    /* 6. constexpr object that can be used at compile‑time */
    constexpr Point p{3, 4};
    static_assert(p.sum() == 7, "Point sum wrong!");

    /* 7. Compile‑time result can be printed at run‑time */
    std::cout << "5! = " << factorial(5) << '\n';

    /* 8. The same function works at run‑time too */
    int n;
    std::cout << "Enter a number to compute factorial: ";
    std::cin >> n;
    std::cout << n << "! = " << factorial(n) << '\n';
}
```

### What this code demonstrates

| Feature | Code snippet | Explanation |
|---------|--------------|-------------|
| Compile‑time array size | `std::array<int, max_size> arr{}` | `max_size` is known at compile time because `factorial(5)` is `constexpr`. |
| Compile‑time check | `static_assert(factorial(4) == 24, ...)` | The condition is evaluated during compilation. |
| `constexpr` class | `constexpr Point p{3, 4};` | The constructor and `sum()` are `constexpr`; `p.sum()` can be used in a `static_assert`. |
| Dual‑use function | `factorial(n)` | Called with a literal (compile‑time) and with user input (run‑time). |
| `constexpr` variable | `constexpr int max_size = factorial(5);` | Holds a compile‑time constant, can be used wherever a constant expression is required. |

---

## Take‑away

* **`constexpr` = “can be evaluated at compile time”.**
* It is a *promise* to the compiler, not a strict restriction (the code can still run at run‑time if needed).
* Use it when you want the compiler to perform the work early, or when you need a value that must be known at compile time (array bounds, template arguments, etc.).
* Modern C++ (C++14+) makes `constexpr` much more powerful: loops, recursion, constructors, and more are allowed.

Happy constexpr‑coding! 🚀
